---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { data, REPO_URL } from "../../lib/data";
import publicQs from "../../data/public-questions.json";

const title = "Methodology: How We Test Local LLMs | Model Tested";
const description = "How Model Tested picks models, writes and hides its test tasks, grades coding and extraction answers, and estimates VRAM and Mac memory requirements.";
const ma = data.memory_assumptions;
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>How we test</h1>
  <p class="lede">Everything here is automated, repeatable and as transparent as we can make it without giving models the answers.</p>

  <h2>Which models</h2>
  <p>
    Every day we check for new open-weight models (public weights on Hugging Face) from the major labs that are small enough to
    run on a single consumer GPU or a Mac — currently up to about 40B total parameters. Safety classifiers and
    translation-only or speech models are excluded.
  </p>

  <h2>The tasks</h2>
  <ul>
    {data.suites.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.name}</strong>: {s.description} {s.n_public + s.n_private} tasks, {s.n_public} of them public.</li>)}
  </ul>
  <h3>Coding</h3>
  <p>
    Each task is a precise spec for a Python function or class. We take the model's code, append our unit tests and run it in
    a sandbox with no network access. A task scores 1 only if every assertion passes; otherwise 0.
  </p>
  <h3>Structured extraction</h3>
  <p>
    The model gets realistic text (invoices, résumés, statements, contracts…) and a list of fields, and must reply with JSON.
    We compare every field with the answer key — case-insensitive for text, ±0.005 for numbers — and divide matched fields by
    all fields in either the answer key or the reply, so invented extra fields cost points too.
  </p>

  <h2>Why most tasks are hidden</h2>
  <p>
    Public benchmarks end up in training data, and then scores measure memory instead of skill. So about three quarters of our
    tasks are private. To keep us honest we publish a sample of tasks, every model's raw answers to them, the grading code,
    and each model's public vs. hidden score side by side — a big gap is a red flag. Hidden tasks are rotated into the
    public set over time.
  </p>

  <h2>Precision</h2>
  <p>
    We query models through OpenRouter, preferring providers that serve full (BF16) or FP8 weights. Most people run 4- to
    6-bit quantized files locally, which typically lose a little quality, so treat our scores as the model's ceiling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="memory">Memory estimates</h2>
  <p>
    We don't measure speed — it depends too much on your exact hardware, runtime and settings. We do estimate whether a model
    fits: <code>parameters × bits-per-weight ÷ 8</code> for the weights, plus an FP16 KV cache for a {ma.context.toLocaleString()}-token
    context (from the model's layer and head counts), plus {ma.overhead_gb} GB of runtime overhead.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {Object.entries(ma.quants).map(([q, bpw]) => <li>{q}: ~{bpw} bits per weight</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Usable memory: VRAM minus 0.5 GB on a GPU; on Apple Silicon, macOS lets the GPU use about two thirds of RAM up to 36 GB
    and three quarters above that by default. Mixture-of-experts models need memory for all their parameters, even though
    only a few are active per token.
  </p>

  <h2>Public sample tasks</h2>
  <p class="meta">{publicQs.canary}</p>
  {publicQs.questions.map((q: any) => (
    <details>
      <summary>{q.suite} · {q.id}</summary>
      {q.suite === "coding" ? (
        <>
          <pre><code>{q.prompt}</code></pre>
          <p class="sub">Unit tests:</p>
          <pre><code>{q.tests.trim()}</code></pre>
        </>
      ) : (
        <>
          <pre><code>{q.text}</code></pre>
          <p class="sub">Fields requested:</p>
          <pre><code>{q.fields}</code></pre>
          <p class="sub">Answer key:</p>
          <pre><code>{JSON.stringify(q.expected, null, 2)}</code></pre>
        </>
      )}
    </details>
  ))}
  {REPO_URL && <p>Harness source and raw outputs: <a href={REPO_URL}>{REPO_URL.replace("https://", "")}</a>.</p>}
</Base>
